  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 221 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: We are currently in the warm sector ahead of an \napproaching cold front with temperatures warming into the mid 50s to \nlower 60s. As of 1 PM, the approaching cold front was located in far \nwestern New York and will continue to quickly push east this \nafternoon and evening. A sharp thermal gradient will allow for \ntemperatures to plummet quickly in the wake of the cold front with \nan abrupt wind direction shift from the south to the northwest. With \nincreasing moisture in the warm sector, we should see rain showers \ncontinue into the evening ahead of the front but rainfall amounts \ndon't look to be anything impressive at this time. As rainfall taper \noff in the wake of the cold front, the northwesterly flow regime \nwill support upslope rain and/or snow showers across the western \nslopes of the Adirondacks and Green Mountains. Our current thinking \nis that locations above 2000 ft may be able to accumulate up to an \ninch of snow while a few flurries could be seen as low as 1500 ft. \nOverall impacts will be minimal and likely confined to grassy \nsurfaces as the ground temperature will remain above freezing \nthrough the period of possible snow.\n\nSaturday will be quite the transition to a more winter-like pattern \nas temperatures will struggle to climb into the 40s while winds \nremain gusty in the 20 to 30 mph range. This will lead to wind \nchills in the 20s and 30s throughout the day with wind chills as low \nas the single digits above and below zero across the high peaks. If \nyou plan to be outdoors tomorrow please dress for the weather as \nthis change in weather pattern may be surprising to many! Saturday \nnight will be the coldest night as winds finally abate and skies \nclear with lows falling into the 20s to near 30 degrees with the \nwarmest values in and around Lake Champlain. A rebound to more \nseasonal normal temperatures is expected for Sunday with highs \nwarming into the 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A rex block will begin to develop for the Northeast as \nsurface high pressure builds in from the west on Monday with a \ndeveloping coastal low across the southeast. High pressure will keep \nany moisture well to our south through the extended period, with \nhigh confidence in an extended dry period. That said, some high \nclouds and cirrus clouds will be possible early next week, \nespecially in southern Vermont. With the extended period of dry \nweather anticipated, and trees losing leaves, fire weather concerns \nmay increase towards the latter portion of next week as we dry out \nfrom this weeks rains early next week. Winds look to be on the weak \nside with light and terrain driven flow under high pressure, which \nshould be beneficial to lowering fire weather concerns. Temperatures \nwill return to normal levels by early next week with highs in the \nupper 50s to low 60s, and overnight lows in the upper 20s to mid 30s.",
